['all_star', 'appearances', 'manager_award', 'player_award', 'manager_award_vote', 'player_award_vote', 'batting', 'batting_postseason', 'player_college', 'fielding', 'fielding_outfield', 'fielding_postseason', 'hall_of_fame', 'home_game', 'manager', 'manager_half', 'player', 'park', 'pitching', 'pitching_postseason', 'salary', 'college', 'postseason', 'team', 'team_franchise', 'team_half']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables, columns and why they matter  
• batting.player_id – foreign key that identifies the hitter  
• batting.g – regular‑season Games Played for the row’s stint / year  
• batting.r – Runs scored for the row’s stint / year  
• batting.h – Hits for the row’s stint / year  
• batting.hr – Home‑Runs for the row’s stint / year  
• player.player_id – primary key that matches batting.player_id  
• player.name_given – player’s “given name(s)” (full first & middle names)  

Typical conditions you will need  
• Aggregate the season lines of the batting table (SUM(g), SUM(r)… GROUP BY player_id) to obtain career totals.  
• Find the maximum value for each stat; e.g. HAVING SUM(g)= (SELECT MAX(total_g) FROM …).  
• Join the aggregated result with the player table to display the name(s).

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (all capped at 20 rows) that move from simple inspection toward the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the batting table: first 20 rows
SELECT *
FROM batting
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct list of player_ids that appear in batting
SELECT DISTINCT player_id
FROM batting
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Who has logged the most regular‑season games? (top 20)
SELECT player_id,
       SUM(g) AS total_games
FROM   batting
GROUP  BY player_id
ORDER  BY total_games DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Top 20 in career runs scored
SELECT player_id,
       SUM(r) AS total_runs
FROM   batting
GROUP  BY player_id
ORDER  BY total_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Top 20 in career hits
SELECT player_id,
       SUM(h) AS total_hits
FROM   batting
GROUP  BY player_id
ORDER  BY total_hits DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Top 20 in career home‑runs
SELECT player_id,
       SUM(hr) AS total_hr
FROM   batting
GROUP  BY player_id
ORDER  BY total_hr DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Join with the player table to get given names for the Games‑Played leaders
SELECT p.name_given,
       b.total_games
FROM  (
        SELECT player_id, SUM(g) AS total_games
        FROM   batting
        GROUP  BY player_id
      ) AS b
JOIN  player AS p
  ON  p.player_id = b.player_id
ORDER BY b.total_games DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join with the player table to get given names for the Runs leaders
SELECT p.name_given,
       b.total_runs
FROM  (
        SELECT player_id, SUM(r) AS total_runs
        FROM   batting
        GROUP  BY player_id
      ) AS b
JOIN  player AS p
  ON  p.player_id = b.player_id
ORDER BY b.total_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Join with the player table to get given names for the Hits leaders
SELECT p.name_given,
       b.total_hits
FROM  (
        SELECT player_id, SUM(h) AS total_hits
        FROM   batting
        GROUP  BY player_id
      ) AS b
JOIN  player AS p
  ON  p.player_id = b.player_id
ORDER BY b.total_hits DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: one row per category showing the player(s) who own the record
WITH career AS (
    SELECT player_id,
           SUM(g)  AS total_g,
           SUM(r)  AS total_r,
           SUM(h)  AS total_h,
           SUM(hr) AS total_hr
    FROM   batting
    GROUP  BY player_id
),
max_vals AS (
    SELECT MAX(total_g)  AS max_g,
           MAX(total_r)  AS max_r,
           MAX(total_h)  AS max_h,
           MAX(total_hr) AS max_hr
    FROM   career
)
SELECT 'Games Played' AS category,
       p.name_given,
       c.total_g       AS value
FROM   career AS c, max_vals AS m
JOIN   player  AS p ON p.player_id = c.player_id
WHERE  c.total_g = m.max_g

UNION ALL
SELECT 'Runs'         AS category,
       p.name_given,
       c.total_r       AS value
FROM   career AS c, max_vals AS m
JOIN   player  AS p ON p.player_id = c.player_id
WHERE  c.total_r = m.max_r

UNION ALL
SELECT 'Hits'         AS category,
       p.name_given,
       c.total_h       AS value
FROM   career AS c, max_vals AS m
JOIN   player  AS p ON p.player_id = c.player_id
WHERE  c.total_h = m.max_h

UNION ALL
SELECT 'Home Runs'    AS category,
       p.name_given,
       c.total_hr      AS value
FROM   career AS c, max_vals AS m
JOIN   player  AS p ON p.player_id = c.player_id
WHERE  c.total_hr = m.max_hr;
```

Each query builds familiarity with the data and culminates in a concise statement that returns the “given name(s)” of the record‑holders together with their record totals for Games Played, Runs, Hits, and Home Runs.
